  <ThreatInstances xmlns:a="http://schemas.microsoft.com/2003/10/Serialization/Arrays">
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-819BDDAC5F</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>1</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Compose service runs privileged</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>ComposeService api runs with `privileged: true`, disabling container isolation entirely. Code execution in this service is equivalent to root on the host.

Evidence: docker-compose.yml:2
privileged: true

Risk 25/25 (likelihood 5 x impact 5). Exposure: 1 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Remove privileged; grant specific cap_add entries instead.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-CMP-001</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Compose service runs privileged</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-CMP-001</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-FEFA5E2ECC</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>2</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Compose service mounts the Docker socket</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>ComposeService api mounts /var/run/docker.sock. This grants full control of the Docker daemon, which is root on the host by another name.

Evidence: docker-compose.yml:2
docker.sock is mounted

Risk 25/25 (likelihood 5 x impact 5). Exposure: 1 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Use a socket proxy with a minimal API allowlist, or rootless Docker.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-CMP-002</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Compose service mounts the Docker socket</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-CMP-002</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-4C0792136E</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>3</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Compose service has plaintext credentials in environment</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Credential-shaped environment variables are set inline in the compose file, which is version-controlled and visible to anyone who can run `docker inspect`.

Evidence: docker-compose.yml:2
credential-like env vars with literal values

Risk 25/25 (likelihood 5 x impact 5). Exposure: 1 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Move values to an untracked env_file or to Docker/Swarm secrets.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-CMP-004</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>e69287b9-e00f-58ba-8346-1e34b50791d2</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Compose service has plaintext credentials in environment</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-CMP-004</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-3D3939228B</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>4</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>32bdd750-ec7f-582c-8f04-72c91da0bfc2</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container runs in privileged mode</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container shop/web runs with `privileged: true`, which disables essentially every container isolation control: all Linux capabilities are granted, /dev is fully exposed, and AppArmor/seccomp are effectively bypassed. An attacker with code execution in this container can mount the host filesystem, write to /etc/kubernetes, read the kubelet credentials, and take over the node -- and from the node, every other pod scheduled on it.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:23
securityContext.privileged is true

Risk 25/25 (likelihood 5 x impact 5). Exposure: 3 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 5.

Remediation: Remove privileged mode and grant only the specific capabilities needed.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>32bdd750-ec7f-582c-8f04-72c91da0bfc2</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-001</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>32bdd750-ec7f-582c-8f04-72c91da0bfc2</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Container runs in privileged mode</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-001</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-A8295E0EEA</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>5</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container mounts the container runtime socket</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Deployment shop/storefront mounts the Docker/containerd/CRI-O socket. This is equivalent to granting root on the node: anyone who can write to the socket can start a new privileged container with the host filesystem mounted. There is no meaningful sandbox left.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:7
hostPath volume includes the runtime socket

Risk 25/25 (likelihood 5 x impact 5). Exposure: 2 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 5.

Remediation: Remove the socket mount; use a rootless build tool or a proxy with an allowlist.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-004</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Container mounts the container runtime socket</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-004</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-E9E02CF5E9</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>6</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>32bdd750-ec7f-582c-8f04-72c91da0bfc2</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Plaintext credential in container environment variables</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Environment variables DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY on Container shop/web contain literal values and are named like credentials. Env vars are visible to anyone with `kubectl describe pod`, are captured in crash dumps and in most APM/error-tracking agents, are inherited by every child process, and are readable from /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/environ by anything sharing the PID namespace.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:23
env vars with literal values: DB_PASSWORD, STRIPE_API_KEY

Risk 25/25 (likelihood 5 x impact 5). Exposure: 3 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 5.

Remediation: Move the value into a Secret and reference it, or mount it as a file.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>32bdd750-ec7f-582c-8f04-72c91da0bfc2</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-015</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>32bdd750-ec7f-582c-8f04-72c91da0bfc2</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Plaintext credential in container environment variables</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-015</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-40054F05CC</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
