        <b:InteractionKey>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container runs as root</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container shop/postgres has no runAsNonRoot/runAsUser constraint, so it runs as UID 0 inside the container. Root in the container means write access to every file in the image, the ability to install tooling at runtime, and a materially easier escape if a kernel or runtime vulnerability is available.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:115
runAsUser is unset or 0

Risk 20/25 (likelihood 4 x impact 5). Exposure: 3 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 6.

Remediation: Run as a dedicated non-root UID.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
