        <b:Id>22</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Pod shares the host network namespace</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Deployment shop/storefront runs with hostNetwork: true. The pod shares the node&#39;s network namespace, so it can sniff traffic destined for other pods on the node, bind to privileged node ports, bypass NetworkPolicy entirely (policies do not apply to host-network pods), and reach the kubelet on localhost:10250.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:7
spec.hostNetwork is true

Risk 16/25 (likelihood 4 x impact 4). Exposure: 2 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 5.

Remediation: Use the pod network and expose the workload through a Service.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-006</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Pod shares the host network namespace</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-006</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-5C511CED45</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>23</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Pod shares the host PID or IPC namespace</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Sharing hostPID lets the container see and signal every process on the node, and read secrets from other processes&#39; /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/environ. Sharing hostIPC exposes shared memory segments belonging to other workloads.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:7
spec.hostPID

Risk 16/25 (likelihood 4 x impact 4). Exposure: 2 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 5.

Remediation: Remove hostPID and hostIPC.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-007</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>b5009d2d-68ab-53d1-bcd7-6bbaad1b988b</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Pod shares the host PID or IPC namespace</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-007</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-0147A11831</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>24</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>750e221b-c157-551f-94be-601b211b7721</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container adds dangerous Linux capabilities</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container shop/sidecar-agent adds capabilities SYS_ADMIN, NET_RAW. SYS_ADMIN is close to full root; SYS_PTRACE allows reading memory of other processes; NET_ADMIN permits traffic redirection and ARP spoofing; SYS_MODULE allows loading kernel modules, which is a direct host takeover.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:41
capabilities.add = SYS_ADMIN, NET_RAW

Risk 16/25 (likelihood 4 x impact 4). Exposure: 3 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 5.

Remediation: Drop ALL, then add back only the minimum.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>750e221b-c157-551f-94be-601b211b7721</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-008</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>750e221b-c157-551f-94be-601b211b7721</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Container adds dangerous Linux capabilities</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-008</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-00CDA7D7B5</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>25</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>b4174d62-3978-51b0-8f7b-286b7f5b11dc</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>NodePort service exposes the workload on every node</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>A NodePort opens the port on every node in the cluster. Whether that is internet-reachable depends entirely on node security groups, which are usually managed by a different team than the manifest -- so this is exposure you cannot verify from the manifest alone.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:129
nodePorts = 30432

Risk 16/25 (likelihood 4 x impact 4). Exposure: 1 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 7.

Remediation: Prefer ClusterIP behind an Ingress; if NodePort is required, restrict node security groups.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>b4174d62-3978-51b0-8f7b-286b7f5b11dc</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-NET-009</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>b4174d62-3978-51b0-8f7b-286b7f5b11dc</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>NodePort service exposes the workload on every node</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-NET-009</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-AF714E2121</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>26</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>81101e02-645f-5d5b-b70d-8ea2126ad784</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Object storage bucket has a public ACL</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>aws_s3_bucket assets is configured with a public-read or public-read-write ACL. Public buckets are found by automated scanners within hours. public-read-write is worse than disclosure: anyone can also overwrite objects, which turns the bucket into a malware distribution point under your domain.

Evidence: tf/main.tf:1
resource aws_s3_bucket with public ACL

Risk 15/25 (likelihood 3 x impact 5). Exposure: unreachable from an external entity. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Set the ACL to private and enable the account-level public access block.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>81101e02-645f-5d5b-b70d-8ea2126ad784</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-CLOUD-001</b:StateInformation>
