        <b:Id>32</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container does not drop all capabilities</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container shop/postgres keeps the default Docker capability set (CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE, FOWNER, SETUID, SETGID, NET_RAW and others). SETUID/SETGID enable local privilege escalation via setuid binaries; NET_RAW enables ARP/DNS spoofing against pods sharing the node network.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:115
capabilities.drop does not include ALL

Risk 12/25 (likelihood 3 x impact 4). Exposure: 3 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 6.

Remediation: Drop ALL capabilities as the baseline.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-009</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Container does not drop all capabilities</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-009</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-22E505D1B7</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>33</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Container root filesystem is writable</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Tampering</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>A writable root filesystem lets an attacker with code execution drop a web shell, overwrite application binaries, or install persistence that survives a process restart. It also removes a cheap tripwire: with a read-only root, most post-exploitation tooling fails immediately and noisily.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:115
readOnlyRootFilesystem is not true

Risk 12/25 (likelihood 3 x impact 4). Exposure: 3 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 6.

Remediation: Make the root filesystem read-only and mount emptyDir for writable paths.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-K8S-010</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>f7981437-198e-5e3b-8dd4-ea64abf6ab07</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Container root filesystem is writable</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-K8S-010</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-C38E0336C5</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
