        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>39</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>61d1449b-9cf2-5df9-8a35-6ae635b61aa3</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Role can write Secrets</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Tampering</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Write access to Secrets allows an attacker to plant credentials that other workloads will trust -- for example replacing a TLS keypair or an image pull secret to redirect pulls to an attacker-controlled registry.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:156
write verbs on secrets: *

Risk 12/25 (likelihood 3 x impact 4). Exposure: 4 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Remove write access; provision Secrets through a controller instead.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>61d1449b-9cf2-5df9-8a35-6ae635b61aa3</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-RBAC-005</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>61d1449b-9cf2-5df9-8a35-6ae635b61aa3</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Role can write Secrets</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-RBAC-005</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-54F847345C</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>40</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>65592bfa-9bb5-5349-a9eb-d9f97ef94596</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>Medium</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Compute instance permits IMDSv1</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>aws_instance worker allows IMDSv1. An SSRF in any application on the instance reaches 169.254.169.254 with a single unauthenticated GET and returns the instance role&#39;s temporary credentials. IMDSv2&#39;s PUT-token requirement blocks the entire SSRF class.

Evidence: tf/main.tf:24
http_tokens is not &#39;required&#39;

Risk 10/25 (likelihood 2 x impact 5). Exposure: unreachable from an external entity. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Require IMDSv2 and lower the hop limit.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Medium</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>65592bfa-9bb5-5349-a9eb-d9f97ef94596</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-CLOUD-009</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>65592bfa-9bb5-5349-a9eb-d9f97ef94596</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Compute instance permits IMDSv1</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-CLOUD-009</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-79E48151A6</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>41</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>ef93f0eb-45ca-54aa-90cc-5f03c5fd6259</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>Medium</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Image runs as root</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>The final stage of DockerImage node:latest has no USER instruction, so the image runs as UID 0. Every downstream consumer inherits that default -- an orchestrator has to opt out explicitly, and most manifests never do.

Evidence: Dockerfile:1
no USER instruction in the final stage

Risk 10/25 (likelihood 2 x impact 5). Exposure: unreachable from an external entity. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Create and switch to a non-root user.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Medium</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>ef93f0eb-45ca-54aa-90cc-5f03c5fd6259</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NotStarted</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>TF-DKR-001</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>ef93f0eb-45ca-54aa-90cc-5f03c5fd6259</b:TargetGuid>
        <b:Title>Image runs as root</b:Title>
        <b:TypeId>TF-DKR-001</b:TypeId>
        <b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded>
      </a:Value>
    </a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
    <a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB>
      <a:Key>TF-34D8EACA5D</a:Key>
      <a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase">
        <b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/>
        <b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>53412b27-c301-5c73-9dcd-cecf78da5cd3</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>
        <b:FlowGuid i:nil="true"/>
        <b:Id>42</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>ef93f0eb-45ca-54aa-90cc-5f03c5fd6259</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>Medium</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Build pipes remote script directly into a shell</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Tampering</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>A `curl ... | sh` pattern executes unverified remote code as root during the build. Whoever controls that URL controls the contents of your image, and the compromise leaves no trace in the Dockerfile.

Evidence: Dockerfile:3
curl/wget piped to a shell

Risk 10/25 (likelihood 2 x impact 5). Exposure: unreachable from an external entity. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Download, verify the checksum, inspect, then execute.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Medium</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
