        <b:Priority>Medium</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Security group allows ingress from 0.0.0.0/0</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>aws_security_group web permits inbound traffic from the entire internet on port(s) 22. If any of those are 22, 3389, 3306, 5432, or 6379 this is immediately exploitable; even for 443 it removes the option of IP-based containment during an incident.

Evidence: tf/main.tf:14
open ports = 22 from 0.0.0.0/0

Risk 8/25 (likelihood 2 x impact 4). Exposure: unreachable from an external entity. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Restrict to known CIDRs, or front the service with a load balancer and WAF.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Medium</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
