        <b:Id>60</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>7f20f78c-6aff-5361-93a2-5f49720a8568</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>Medium</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Credential-like values stored in a ConfigMap</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>ConfigMap shop/shop-config holds keys API_TOKEN that look like credentials. ConfigMaps get none of the (already modest) protections Secrets receive: they are not candidates for encryption at rest by default, are routinely granted broad read access, and are frequently dumped wholesale into logs and support bundles.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:90
credential-like keys: API_TOKEN

Risk 8/25 (likelihood 2 x impact 4). Exposure: unreachable from an external entity. Blast radius 0.

Remediation: Move these keys into a Secret or an external secret store, and rotate them.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
