        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Service account token automounted but unused API access</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Elevation of Privilege</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>automountServiceAccountToken is unset, so a projected API token is mounted at /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount. If the workload does not call the Kubernetes API, this token is pure attack surface -- it is the first thing post-exploitation tooling looks for.

Evidence: k8s/app.yaml:99
automountServiceAccountToken is unset (defaults to true)

Risk 3/25 (likelihood 1 x impact 3). Exposure: 2 hop(s) from the internet. Blast radius 6.

Remediation: Set automountServiceAccountToken false unless the pod calls the API server.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
